{"round": 1, "phase": "full", "verdict": "ISSUES_FOUND", "blocking": true, "verifier_model": "gpt-5.4", "verifier_provider": "openai", "orchestrator_provider": "anthropic", "findings": [{"description": "The API freshness check collapses all vendors to one record-level timestamp, so a partial enumeration failure still reads as fresh.", "severity": "major", "category": "Correctness", "failureScenario": "On the shipped three-provider config, one vendor key expires or one models endpoint is down while another vendor still answers. `enumerate` records the failure, but `merge_record()` advances `meta.enumerated_at` as soon as any provider succeeds, and `status` / `session start` only read that single timestamp. The API record is therefore reported fresh even though one provider's entries are stale. Partial provider failure is an expected operational path here, not an edge case.", "evidencePaths": ["ai_router/discovery.py", "ai_router/discovery.py", "ai_router/discovery.py", "ai_router/discovery.py", "ai_router/session.py", "ai_router/router-config.yaml"], "blocking": true, "section": "body"}, {"description": "The new API record is written inside `ai_router/` and explicitly published as package data, so the \"local, regenerated, not shipped\" invariant is not implemented.", "severity": "major", "category": "Completeness", "failureScenario": "A maintainer runs `python -m ai_router.discovery enumerate` in the repo and then builds a package from that same tree. The command writes `ai_router/api-models.lock`, and `pyproject.toml` opts that filename into package data, so the build can ship a key-set-local discovery record to every consumer. That is a normal workflow for this feature, not a contrived misuse.", "evidencePaths": ["ai_router/router-config.yaml", "ai_router/discovery.py", "ai_router/discovery.py", "pyproject.toml"], "blocking": true, "section": "body"}], "completion_tree": "3c9be74cca16d1d82a20152d8d9ea7e867661066", "recorded_at": "2026-08-27T09:45:01.000673-04:00",